A||||14||saleenfiero||FIERO VS. THE WORLD:  Braking Comparison||||1||
Z||000000||saleenfiero||06-02-2001||11:15 AM||||Anybody wanna comment on how good or bad the fieros brakes are compared to others?  I was actually wondering about my in-laws 91 or 92 Z28 convertible with its disc/drum setup among other cars....||||reg||1
Z||000001||btoth||06-02-2001||12:46 PM||||I hate my Fiero's brakes.  I was driving my parent's Z3 for a month while I was getting the Fiero's engine replaced and its brakes stop on a dime with no effort at all.  My parent's '92 Nissan Maxima is somewhere in the middle between my car and the BMW.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000002||88formula||06-02-2001||02:42 PM|||| I owned an 87 coupe and had to stand on the brake pedal to get it to stop quickly but my 88 stopped very well and the pads looked like this when I removed them.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Rusted_brake_pad_resized_1.JPG">  Can't wait to see how it stops now.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/88formula_logo_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||Shiner||06-02-2001||05:31 PM||||Btoth,<BR>  It's unfair to compare ANY new BMW's brakes to the Fiero's.  I had the chance to really wring out a couple different new bimmers, and even the 3 series has incredible brakes.||||reg||1
Z||000004||saleenfiero||06-02-2001||06:41 PM||||As much as I believe that an Z3 could smoke a Fiero in braking (and just about anything else), I was more think along the lines of early 90's cars and in the "fiero" years...||||reg||1
Z||000005||ElricSI||06-02-2001||08:33 PM||||I used to own an 86 CRX and as I remember, that sucka could really stop.  Sometimes I worry the Fiero won't stop in time.  But so far it always has.  (Knock on wood.)<P>------------------<BR>Proud Owner of 85 GT||||reg||1
Z||000006||PBJ||06-02-2001||10:00 PM||||I have found quality of brake pads make the differance for me. I bought a cheap set last time I did brakes and brakes were worse than the worn out one's. so I took them off and put on GM pads and have good brakes again.  You usually get what you pay for I guess.||||reg||1
Z||000007||fiero56||06-02-2001||10:07 PM||||I haven't driven another car that has better brakes than my '88. I had sem-metallics on before, and they sucked. So I slapped some organic pads on, and it will stop faster than anything I have ever driven. <P>The brakes on my '86...suck. They aren't good, they're not bad, they do the job. I don't know how good the system is right now though.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Nashco||06-02-2001||11:36 PM||||I have some very real world comparisons <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  On my three cars, I've at one time or another performed a "brake test" to see how quick it would stop if I absolutely had to slam on them.<P>My 69 VW bug, with fully operational 4-wheel drum brakes stopped alright, but not nearly as much as I'd like.  It is slightly lighter than stock, due to all fiberglass hoods and fenders.  I did have much heavier/larger tires and wheels because it was a baja, so the brakes were just right for stopping at max potential without locking up the wheels.<P>My 80 Honda Accord LX, with fully operation front disks and rear drums, stops awesome.  It stops way faster than you would expect a car of that vintage to stop.  It won't lock up the brakes on warm, dry days, but will other days, so it's just right for maximum stopping.  The car is completely stock, with 165/75/13 tires.<P>My 88 GT stops better than the Honda, but just barely.  I attribute some of this to a wider tire, thus the ability to brake harder on dry days.  I also attribute it to less body roll/tighter suspension, so there is less reliance on just the front brakes.  I am very pleased with the GT's brakes, but I know there's more potential in them.  I haven't done anything to the brakes since I bought them, so I have no idea what kind of pads are in.  I am going to get 17's within the next year, and plan on putting in much bigger brakes after that.  I CAN'T WAIT <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  I'm a G freak....I started out with this car so I could autocross...and I don't plan on backing out of that idea.<P>Bryce<BR>88 GT <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000009||88FORMULA-T-TOP-GUY||06-03-2001||01:35 AM||||I beleive I saw in an old Car and Driver reprint that the 88 Fiero had 60 to 0 braking distance of 145 ft which is within 5 feet + or - of all of the "super cars" and up to 40 ft shorter than all of the wanna-be sports cars.  As for my 88 Formula with Dunlops, it brakes very evenly dry or wet and will throw you through the windshield if you stand on the brakes hard. It does take a lot of pressure though.||||reg||1
Z||000010||AusFiero||06-03-2001||04:23 AM||||Fieros have brakes?<P>The following has been done to my 86 GT and it does not brake well.<BR>2 new front rotors.<BR>2 rear rotors machined.<BR>Factory recall kit fitted to rear calipers.<BR>Re-conditioned front caplipers.<BR>New brake pads all round. GM replacement parts.<BR>Master Cylinder re-condititoned.<BR>Proportional valve checked and verified ok.<P>So does anyone have any ideas why mine still locks the fronts under heavy braking?<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.jadeweb.com.au/fiero/fiero.gif"> <BR><A HREF="http://www.jadeweb.com.au/fiero" TARGET=_blank>My Fiero</A><BR>When I was young I spent 80% of my money on Women, Gambling and Alcohol. I guess I wasted the rest.||||reg||10
Z||000011||fiero56||06-03-2001||08:42 AM||||The fronts are supposed to lock first.||||reg||1
Z||000012||Fierowrecker||06-03-2001||09:10 AM||||Hey Aus!<BR>If you can lock the fronts, then I say, you need wider/stickier tires...<BR>You could also get a taller tire/rim combination which would decrease the braking efficiency...<BR>crash... ||||reg||1
Z||000013||Pontiaddict||06-03-2001||07:51 PM||||Fiero brakes are weird. When you press them like you would normally do for a stop, they suck. When you rally stand on them, they seem to do their job though. It was the same with both the 84 I had and my 86GT. The actual design of the brakes is bad in my opinion. They are the same design as the ones on my old 86 Trans Am and I hate them. For that reason, I would upgrade them as soon as I can.||||reg||1
Z||000014||MrPBody||06-03-2001||08:01 PM||||My '85 SE brakes stank (metaphorically) until I replaced the front pads with CarboTech Super Street.  Now they're great -- way better than my 1958 Volvo.  (Not saying much.)<BR>||||reg||1
